**Alternative Meeting Summary: Content Production and Distribution Review** **Date:** March 30, 2024 **Time:** 1:00 PM - 2:30 PM **Location:** Conference Room C, MediaCorp Headquarters, Los Angeles, CA **Attendees:** - John Smith, Chief Content Officer, MediaCorp - Sarah Johnson, VP of Broadcasting, MediaCorp - James Lee, Head of Streaming Services, MediaCorp - Maria Garcia, Senior Marketing Director, MediaCorp - David Chen, Data Analytics Specialist, MediaCorp - Lisa Wong, Creative Director, MediaCorp - Tom Robinson, Advertising Manager, AdVantage Solutions - Rachel Adams, Legal Advisor, MediaCorp - Mark Thompson, Head of Production, MediaCorp - Emily Nguyen, Social Media Strategist, MediaCorp **Agenda:** 1. Examination of production delays and their impact on content release 2. Assessment of declining viewership on key streaming platforms 3. Review of advertising partnerships and unexpected revenue declines 4. Legal challenges regarding existing influencer contracts 5. Immediate action items and strategic pivots **Discussion Points:** - John Smith initiated the meeting by exrpessing concerns over the recent documentary series "Voices of Change," which has resulted in a surprising decline in engagement, reported at 15%. - James Lee highlighted significant contractions in viewership on platforms like Hulu and Amazon Prime, prompting an urgent need to overhaul our content syndication strategy to counteract these losses. - Sarah Johnson stated that maintaining our broadcasting schedule has become increasingly challenging, particularly with the approaching premiere of "The Next Big Star" on April 20, 2024, which is now anticipated to draw fewer than 500,000 live viewers due to increased competition and digital fatigue among audiences. - Maria Garcia disclosed that recent advertising metrics revealed a substantial 30% drop in ad impressions, attributing the downturn to ineffective collaborations with AdVantage Solutions and a broader market shift away from traditional TV ads. - Rachel Adams raised critical issues regarding possible non-compliance with evolving FTC regulations related to influencer partnerships, suggesting that several existing contracts might need urgent review to avoid legal repercussions.